    login path, tracking silently misses it — which defeats the point.
  - [3mComplexity:[0m moderate.

  [1mC. A [38;5;153mloginAndTrack[39m wrapper composing the two.[0m Gets both properties — separated internals, hard to forget at the call
  site.
  - [3mDiscarding this one:[0m it's three functions and an indirection layer for a 28-line file with a single caller. YAGNI.
    Worth revisiting only if a second login path appears.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m Your phrasing — "so we can track who logged in" — reads as an invariant, not a convenience, and A is
  the only one of the two that enforces it. The coupling cost is real but cheap to unwind later (lifting the tracking
  call out of [38;5;153mlogin[39m into the caller is a five-line change); silently-missed login events are not cheap to detect. B is
  the better answer the moment this file grows a second entry point.

  Two details that apply either way, and I want them visible rather than buried in the spec:

  - [1mTracking failure must never fail login.[0m A user who authenticated successfully is logged in, full stop, even if the
    analytics POST 500s. The tracking call gets its own [38;5;153m.catch[39m.
  - [1mThe tracking endpoint URL doesn't exist yet.[0m [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m, a placeholder. I'll add
    [38;5;153mTRACKING_ENDPOINT[39m as a sibling constant and have [38;5;153mtrackLogin[39m no-op when it's unset, so the code is correct and inert
    until you fill in a real URL. That goes in the spec as [38;5;153mAssumption: tracking endpoint URL to be supplied, validate [39m
    [38;5;153mvia <you providing it>[39m.
